WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:03.470
2021 was not a fresh start.

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:07.730
A sampling of newspapers’ front pages, a
stroll through single-digit cable channels,

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:12.890
a scroll through your trusted news sites all
advance the same sentiment: a new year doesn’t

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:17.329
mean a new world, a fresh fourth digit doesn’t
refresh the headlines.

00:00:17.329 --> 00:00:22.519
Rather, 2021 was a year of news stories evolving,
of headlines maturing.

00:00:22.519 --> 00:00:26.260
Pandemic stories went from panic and uncertainty
over the virus to panic and uncertainty over

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:31.139
the vaccine and its roll out; stories on paper-product
shortages turned into headlines outlining

00:00:31.139 --> 00:00:35.840
outright supply chain chaos; coverage of police
violence, racial injustice, and subsequent

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.769
protests moved from the streets and into the
courtroom.

00:00:38.769 --> 00:00:42.679
The strongest point of continuity from last
year to this year, however, was that a host

00:00:42.679 --> 00:00:47.160
of stories burned so bright that they left
little oxygen for smaller ones to breathe.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.691
So, we did it again, we assembled a list of
news stories, one for each country, that you

00:00:51.691 --> 00:00:54.120
likely missed in 2021.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.739
Some rules are the same from last year.

00:00:55.739 --> 00:00:59.379
We’re not covering the big stories—you
won’t hear about America ending its longest

00:00:59.379 --> 00:01:03.510
war, the traffic jam in the Suez Canal, and
you certainly won’t hear me utter the all-consuming

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:04.629
C-word.

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:09.271
In its absence, you’ll notice that two other
c-words came to dominate our headlines: climate

00:01:09.271 --> 00:01:11.180
change, and coup d'etats.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:15.310
These themes point to the change in this year’s
video—rather than focusing on finding stories

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:19.890
distant from the C-word, this edition’s
headlines reflect the experience of each country’s

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:24.020
general populace—stories that often revolve
around responses to a rapidly changing world

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.260
and unnervingly unstable governments.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:30.380
The stories aren’t all dark though–2021
was tough, but it was also one filled with

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.680
the heartening and, of course, the quirky.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.899
Not only does this people-first focus inform
the stories we chose, but also how we organized

00:01:36.899 --> 00:01:38.700
the stories themselves.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.700
Rather than going from most populous to least
like last year, we’re using the Human Development

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:43.850
Index or HDI.

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:48.759
HDI is a metric aimed to quantify standard
of living, ranking each country between 0

00:01:48.759 --> 00:01:53.549
and 1 based on three dimensions: a long and
happy life, accumulation of education, and

00:01:53.549 --> 00:01:54.549
earnings.

00:01:54.549 --> 00:01:58.039
The United Nations compiles this list using
an algorithm that includes indicators such

00:01:58.039 --> 00:02:02.329
as life expectancy at birth, gross national
income per capita, and mean years of schooling

00:02:02.329 --> 00:02:04.320
for adults 25 and over.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.070
HDI was created to, as the UN states, “emphasize
that people and their capabilities should

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:13.360
be the ultimate criteria for assessing the
development of a country, not economic growth

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:14.360
alone.”

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:18.471
Put simply, HDI is the best metric available
to frame this trip around the globe from the

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:20.300
perspective of everyday people.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:26.280
But not all of the 196 UN-recognized countries
and territories we cover have an HDI score.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.610
Missing statistics in per capita income, life
expectancy, or years of education means that

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:34.640
seven countries didn’t have a score, but
they did make news, so we’ll start there,

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.050
and then go from lowest HDI to highest.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.130
We’ve spent a lot of time explaining, but
you’re here for the headlines.

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:43.350
So let’s get started with the first 98.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:47.970
We start this year in the country that the
HDI calculators probably know the least about:

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:48.970
North Korea.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:53.060
The Hermit Kingdom countered its natural disposition
when it decided to reopen lines of communication

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:56.890
with South Korea via the hotline that directly
connects Seoul and Pyongyang.

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:00.920
The hotline’s existed in one form or another
since the 1970s but has been severed twice

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:05.010
in the past two years on account of the failed
Trump summit with the north, and joint military

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.150
exercises between the US and the south.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:11.720
From nations we’d like to know more about
to continents that spark curiosity, Venturi,

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:15.800
an innovative auto company and Formula E team
from Monaco, announced its plan to launch

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.280
its electric polar exploration vehicle in
Antarctica.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:21.450
While the world’s been sending vehicles
down there for decades, Venturi’s will be

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:25.630
the first that spews zero emissions as it
transports scientists across the continent

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:27.660
to conduct important climate-related research.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:31.740
While it’s been tested on French ski resorts,
the sole focus has been getting the vehicle

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:36.240
ready for the southern continent–a mission
laid bare by the vehicle’s name, “Antarctica.”

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.910
The news out of Nauru suggested that 2021
wasn’t one to critically rethink its international

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:43.080
agreements but a chance to double down.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.360
At least that was the case when the country
renewed its deal with Australia to serve as

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:50.750
an offshore processing location for asylum
seekers looking to enter the popular migration

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:51.750
destination.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:55.010
While the extension keeps the island nation
in the good graces of regional power broker

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:59.710
Australia, it keeps the island connected to
and complicit in an asylum system that many,

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:04.460
including the UN, have decried as cruel and
inhuman with its delays, decrepit living conditions,

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:06.000
and reports of abuse.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.960
2021 was an Olympics year, and one of the
smallest countries in the world took two podiums:

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.891
San Marino, a 23-square mile, 60-square kilometer
enclave surrounded by Italy, earned its first

00:04:15.891 --> 00:04:16.949
Olympic medal.

00:04:16.949 --> 00:04:20.780
Women’s trap shooter Alessandra Perilli
took home a bronze during the summer games.

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:25.699
But it wasn’t just her award—now the country,
with 33,000 people, came in first for being

00:04:25.699 --> 00:04:30.110
the least populated territory in the world
to earn an Olympic medal, surpassing Bermuda’s

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:31.110
70,000.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:35.150
Somalia got its first taste of the silver
screen in 30 years, when the National Theatre

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:39.590
of Somalia reopened for a screening featuring
two short films by a homegrown director.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:43.740
The theater closed in 1991 at the start of
the country’s civil war, has been used frequently

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:48.389
by warlords as their headquarters, and was
then bombed by anti-entertainment jihadists.

00:04:48.389 --> 00:04:54.000
Its reopening, though tenuous and under heavy
security, marked a flicker of hope for civilians—especially

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.030
the flick-loving ones.

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:58.530
And we’re not even into the list but we’re
already at climate change.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:02.919
Tuvalu’s foreign minister went viral for
addressing the COP26 climate summit knee-deep

00:05:02.919 --> 00:05:06.889
in water–a strong image that demonstrates
the peril facing the island nation which only

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:09.979
rises to 15 feet, or 4.5 meters, above sea
level.

00:05:09.979 --> 00:05:14.120
He’s asking that if the nation goes underwater,
it can retain ownership to its maritime zones

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:18.590
and rights as a state even if residents have
to relocate–existential questions for a

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:21.349
very non-existential crisis.

00:05:21.349 --> 00:05:25.150
In a less systemic and more personal response
to climbing temperatures, The Vatican’s

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:29.930
most famous resident donated 15,000 ice-creams
to local prisoners during an unpleasant hot

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:33.330
snap in a summer that set heat records across
the Italian peninsula.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:37.350
While the gesture was admittedly minor, it
signaled the Vatican’s dedication to aid

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.469
the local population down on its luck.

00:05:39.469 --> 00:05:43.520
The Vatican’s charity office has also provided
free testing and vaccines during the pandemic

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.539
and has taken locals experiencing homelessness
out for pizza.

00:05:47.539 --> 00:05:52.490
In what's been a tough year for a myriad of
reasons, Niger made history with its first-ever

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:55.469
peaceful, democratic transition of power.

00:05:55.469 --> 00:06:00.080
Once President Mahamadou Issoufou relinquished
the position after two terms—the limit outlined

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:05.419
in the country's constitution—Niger elected
and placed it's new President, Mohamed Bazoum.

00:06:05.419 --> 00:06:12.110
While claims of fraud, protest, outright violence,
and a stymied coup nearly derailed it, 2021’s

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:17.680
successful transition of power was the country's
first since it gained independence in 1960.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.780
Just as Niger elected its new leader, the
Central African Republic lost one of their

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:22.780
own… which the world found out about over
social media.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:26.090
Heavily criticized for the government’s
inability to foster peace among ethnic groups,

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:30.030
it’s worrying ties with Russia, and its
bungled relationship with France who have

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.189
now pulled their military aid out of the country,
Firmin Ngrebada announced that he and his

00:06:34.189 --> 00:06:37.759
government had handed over their resignation
on Twitter.

00:06:37.759 --> 00:06:44.050
Just North, in Chad, 2021 saw both the suspension
and reinstatement of the Chadian Football

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:45.819
Association by the hands of FIFA.

00:06:45.819 --> 00:06:49.749
Citing government interference in Chad’s
effort to seize the Association’s authority

00:06:49.749 --> 00:06:54.340
and training grounds, an unsurprising overstep
under the ruling military council, soccer’s

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:57.419
ruling body banned the country from international
tournaments.

00:06:57.419 --> 00:07:02.050
While restrictions were ultimately lifted,
Chad was disqualified from the all-important

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:04.860
2021 Africa Cup of Nations.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:09.419
The world’s youngest country, South Sudan,
rang in its 10th anniversary with a first,

00:07:09.419 --> 00:07:13.550
as the mobile provider, Digitel, launched
in the capital city of Juba.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:17.610
Digitel is the first South Sudanese-owned
and operated mobile network and its stated

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:22.819
goal is to roll out 4G in Juba at competitive
rates and provide mobile service for remote

00:07:22.819 --> 00:07:27.229
areas—a meaningful step in the country’s
development.

00:07:27.229 --> 00:07:30.970
Important infrastructure developments might
be afoot in nearby Burundi as well.

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:35.210
In July, the heads of state from Burundi and
the Democratic Republic of the Congo signed

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:39.650
cooperative agreements aimed to foster trade,
stamp out rebels, and literally bridge the

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:43.569
Ruzizi River-sized gap between the neighboring
countries.

00:07:43.569 --> 00:07:47.830
Expanding rail lines and electrification along
the borderlands are also key components of

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:49.039
the agreements.

00:07:49.039 --> 00:07:53.759
These deals signal on paper, what it’s leaders
claim have always connected the two countries––in

00:07:53.759 --> 00:07:57.509
their words, a historic “brotherhood.”

00:07:57.509 --> 00:08:01.919
Nonuplets is the crossword-worthy term that
reached household-level parlance across Mali

00:08:01.919 --> 00:08:03.509
in 2021.

00:08:03.509 --> 00:08:08.169
Backed by nation-wide well-wishes, 25-year-old
Halima Cissé was sent to a specialist in

00:08:08.169 --> 00:08:12.289
Morocco in late March to deliver seven babies––or
so she thought.

00:08:12.289 --> 00:08:17.370
To her doctor’s surprise, Cissé harbored
nine, all of which were born in good health.

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:22.279
Cissé’s nonuplets now mark the record for
most babies born at once.

00:08:22.279 --> 00:08:27.990
As the world warms, cities are getting hot––sometimes
deadly hot.

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:31.360
This is the case in Freetown, the capital
city of Sierra Leone.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:35.780
In the face of deadly heat waves, the city
has appointed an extreme heat official to

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:40.320
draft a comprehensive heat strategy, raise
general awareness, and create ways to protect

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.220
the city’s most exposed.

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:46.720
Freetown’s extreme heat official is not
just the first in Africa, but only the third

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:51.850
such official in the world, joining those
recently appointed in Miami and Athens.

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:53.231
Inspiration takes many forms.

00:08:53.231 --> 00:08:59.010
For Burkina Faso nationals, it took the form
of the massive, smiling “Iron Biby,” who

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:01.920
set a new world record in log lifting.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.960
While Western media might have overlooked
his 504-pound-over-the-head-lift in Glasgow,

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:11.801
Scotland, his triumph was big news in Burkina
Faso, as residents welcomed him back at the

00:09:11.801 --> 00:09:13.480
airport in droves.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:17.530
The moment wasn’t lost on “Iron Biby,”
who dedicated his trophy to his home country

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:20.640
as “a message of hope.”

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:24.550
On the other side of Africa, in Mozambique,
researchers have found that elephants have

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:27.690
begun ditching their tusks in the name of
survival.

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:33.010
Once rare, tuskless African elephants have
become increasingly common over the last 50

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:35.510
years––largely, on account of poachers.

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:39.220
With 90% of the country’s elephants killed
for their ivory during Mozambique’s civil

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:44.230
war, tuskless elephants benefitted from an
increased opportunity to pass down their tuskless

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:45.300
genes.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:49.050
With poaching down and conservation up in
recent decades, though, it’s expected that

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:51.280
tusks will come back in vogue.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:55.520
If you’re looking for a reminder that the
past is very much part of the present, look

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:57.560
no further than Eritrea.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:02.840
Over 300 Eritreans born during Italy’s rule
have demanded Italian citizenship.

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:07.370
In the seven-odd decades since colonial rule,
Eritrean claims to Italian citizenship based

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:10.870
on paternal bloodlines have fallen on deaf
ears.

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:16.200
Racial rules during the fascist period, current
Italian immigration policy, lack of documentation,

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:21.330
and limited mobility under Eritrea’s dictatorial
rule have kept an estimated thousands of Eritreans

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:23.730
from accessing Italian citizenship.

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:29.140
The latest push for recognition, though, shows
these claims aren’t fading quietly.

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:33.530
Elegant, meticulously groomed and arranged
trees and bushes aren’t likely what one

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:36.180
would expect along the war-torn streets of
Yemen.

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:40.420
But that’s what you’ll run into when passing
through the city of Marib, where trees sculpted

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:43.220
into shapes, figures, and animals line roadways.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:47.621
In a region of critical strategic importance,
representing one of the government’s last

00:10:47.621 --> 00:10:53.240
surviving strongholds, this city beautification
project, begun in 2016, has offered a welcome

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:56.620
respite from the country’s dragging civil
war.

00:10:56.620 --> 00:11:01.990
“Dragging” also describes the legacy of
the West African Ebola outbreak, as the repercussions

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:06.810
of the epidemic that “ended” in 2016 may
still be taking shape.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:11.060
Researchers suspect that this year’s Ebola
outbreak of 20-plus probable cases in Guinea

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:13.540
was triggered by a survivor of the earlier
outbreak.

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:18.070
While scientists knew that in the rarest of
cases the virus could shed and transmit up

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:22.820
to 500 days after infection, the finding that
a body harbored the virus for a full five

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:26.920
years was a surprising and frightening development.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:29.340
Fifty thousand dollars still goes a long way.

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:34.050
In Liberia, $50,000, and doing the right thing
with it, will earn you a chance to meet the

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:35.330
president.

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:39.970
That was the case for Emmanuel Tuloe, a teenage
motorcycle taxi driver, who found a plastic

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:44.310
bag on the side of the road stuffed with fifty
grand in cash and returned it in full to the

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:46.220
businesswoman who had lost it.

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:50.470
His good deed was praised by Liberians and
earned him the chance to meet the president,

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:54.930
with whom he wanted to discuss getting himself
and fellow youngsters back in school.

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:59.590
For the first time, male green sea turtles
off the coast of Guinea-Bissau are getting

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:04.880
GPS tags to better understand the endangered
species’ travel and feeding patterns.

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:09.520
Not only is the attention a boon for the in-trouble
turtles, it signals the growing trust between

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:14.120
outside researchers and notoriously protective
island residents who have successfully defended

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:17.850
and worshipped this sacred environment for
centuries.

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:22.250
Researchers have tread lightly and, in turn,
benefitted mightily from shared local knowledge

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:26.380
and expertise to catch and tag the ranging
reptiles.

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:30.140
Finding a middle-ground built on trust and
respect has also shaped an important development

00:12:30.140 --> 00:12:34.200
in the Democratic Republic of the Congo as
calls for the repatriation of artifacts by

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:37.210
activists and officials made headway in 2021.

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:41.600
In the wake of the global Black Lives Matter
movement, the Belgian government released

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:46.570
their plan to return all illegitimately procured
African artifacts, most of which were stolen

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:47.790
from the Congo.

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:52.720
This plan marks the first systematic, government-led
effort to return objects stolen during the

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:55.920
country’s violent colonization on the continent.

00:12:55.920 --> 00:13:01.120
Across several Malawian cities, protesters
took to the street bonded by the same plight—data

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:03.800
prices are too darn high.

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:09.630
It seems a fair frustration, as one gigabyte
costs nearly $28, making it the third-most

00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:13.220
expensive rate of all UN-recognized countries.

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:16.990
Malawi regulators have expressed their concern
over exorbitant rates as the prices threaten

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:19.820
to slow social-economic development.

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:25.590
The stories of two prominent headline-makers
collided this year when Tedros Adhanom, director-general

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:30.460
of the World Health Organization, spoke on
the dire situation in north Ethiopia.

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:35.120
In November, the director-general said that
the Tigray region of Ethiopia was under a

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:39.020
“systematic blockade” and that people
were dying because of the lack of supplies.

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:42.790
He also noted that Tigrayans were being arrested
en masse.

00:13:42.790 --> 00:13:47.290
From Tigray himself, Adhanom never directly
assigned blame as he’s worked diligently

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:52.200
to maintain a neutral position on the civil
war that has consumed his home country.

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:56.840
As part of their seemingly odd and certainly
confusing court case against Myanmar, The

00:13:56.840 --> 00:13:59.100
Gambia won an important ruling this year.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:04.430
On behalf of the Rohingya, a Muslim ethnic
group persecuted in Myanmar, the tiny Gambia,

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:09.010
with the backing of the Organization of Islamic
Cooperation, a group of 57 nation-states,

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:12.190
filed a lawsuit against Myanmar in 2019.

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:16.240
In September of this year, a judge ordered
Facebook to disclose account records pertaining

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:20.500
to the persecution in Myanmar––a big win
for The Gambia, as it’s widely understood

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:24.470
that social media played a key role in the
2017 genocide.

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:29.010
Sudan’s progress toward democratic rule
hit a major speed bump this October when the

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:31.050
military seized power.

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:35.950
Since the ousting of Omar al-Bashir in 2019,
Sudan had been trending in the democratic

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:38.930
direction––that was, until this most recent
coup.

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:43.410
In response to the backslide, thousands of
protestors hit the streets in Khartoum, while

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:48.800
the US suspended a $700 million aid package
meant to aid in the transition towards civilian

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:49.800
governance.

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:53.800
The story is still developing though, as the
US and UN are pressuring the military to release

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:57.710
imprisoned politicians and return a civilian-government
to power.

00:14:57.710 --> 00:15:00.220
Democracy is in trouble in Haiti, too.

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:05.550
After a deadly earthquake, a presidential
assasination, and bout after bout of gang

00:15:05.550 --> 00:15:10.750
violence, chaos and uncertainty are primed
to carry into 2022 as general elections have

00:15:10.750 --> 00:15:12.990
been postponed for the fourth time.

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:17.040
This postponement comes after the prime minister
decided to dissolve the council responsible

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:18.920
for organizing elections.

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:23.970
The PM has said he plans on naming a new council,
but has not committed to a timeline––a

00:15:23.970 --> 00:15:26.680
move that leaves the spiraling state, well,
spiraling.

00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:31.830
One hundred and thirty young female footballers
and their families from Afghanistan can finally

00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:33.470
take a deep breath.

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:38.500
Four months after fleeing Kabul as the Taliban
closed in, and after stops in Portugal and

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:42.780
Pakistan, the Afghan athletes arrived in London
via chartered flight.

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:47.290
Their escape was made possible by a litany
of NGOs and philanthropists, one of which

00:15:47.290 --> 00:15:51.440
was the chairman of Leeds United––the
club where the girls will join the youth team

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:52.580
ranks.

00:15:52.580 --> 00:15:58.860
2021 was a big year for African astronomy––especially
for the Senegalese scientist Maram Kaire,

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:04.890
as the International Astronomical Union retitled
the less-than-personable asteroid 35462 to

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:06.930
“MaramKaire,” no spaces.

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:11.150
Not only making the asteroid hurtling between
Mars and Jupiter more approachable, the renaming

00:16:11.150 --> 00:16:15.410
serves as a recognition of the life’s work
of the director of Africaspace and founding

00:16:15.410 --> 00:16:20.020
member of the African Astronomical Society,
while also shining a light on the nation’s

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:21.580
science ambitions.

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:25.340
Senegal doesn’t hold a monopoly on Western
African ambition, though.

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:29.940
Bureaucrats in Togo took the difficult task
of distributing pandemic-related relief funds

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:34.480
head on this year by developing a remarkably
efficient digital payout platform.

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:39.430
With only spotty census data and flawed tax
records, officials creatively leveraged voter

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:44.640
registration to verify applicants before paying
out the aid over SMS––a program that makes

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:47.660
the US’s look antiquated by comparison.

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:51.340
The violence that’s gripped Ethiopia for
the past year has done no favors for East

00:16:51.340 --> 00:16:55.820
African neighbors either––see the tiny
coastal nation of Djibouti.

00:16:55.820 --> 00:17:00.490
In July, protestors responding to a state-led
attack blocked the rail line that connects

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:02.950
the landlocked Ethiopia to the ocean.

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:08.350
Djibouti’s port, through which a whopping
90% of all Ethiopian goods pass, is the country’s

00:17:08.350 --> 00:17:12.360
key economic driver, and while the rail line’s
closure wasn’t permanent, instability in

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:17.611
Ethiopia has hampered the reliant Djibouti
economy to the tune of an estimated $1.7 billion

00:17:17.611 --> 00:17:19.620
dollars in lost commerce.

00:17:19.620 --> 00:17:23.910
It’s been a tough few years for the ski
industry—and Lesotho’s Afriski Mountain

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:28.360
Resort proved that while unique in terms of
its status as one of the two resorts in southern

00:17:28.360 --> 00:17:31.190
Africa, it wasn’t an exception to the struggles.

00:17:31.190 --> 00:17:35.860
Rather than go under when South African border
closures led to a 90% drop in visitors, the

00:17:35.860 --> 00:17:41.169
resort adapted, this year offering incentives
and reduced rates to get locals on the hill—some,

00:17:41.169 --> 00:17:45.399
for the very first time—while also finding
creative renovation projects to keep its local

00:17:45.399 --> 00:17:46.960
staff employed.

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:50.370
Chameleons, by their very nature, are hard
to spot.

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:55.789
A chameleon measuring just 29 millimeters
from tip to tail—nearly impossible.

00:17:55.789 --> 00:18:00.499
And yet, a group of scientists in northern
Madagascar discovered a pair of these nano-chameleons

00:18:00.499 --> 00:18:05.840
who are now in the record books as the smallest
of the planet’s 11,500 reptiles.

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:10.269
Like so much in Madagascar, though, the future
of the little lizard is up in the air as habitat

00:18:10.269 --> 00:18:14.480
loss has conservationists scrambling to protect
the new finding.

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:19.179
From new findings, to expensive and rare findings,
Petra Diamonds, the British producer of pink

00:18:19.179 --> 00:18:24.590
diamonds donned by the Queen herself, has
settled to pay 4.3 million pounds to 71 Tanzanian

00:18:24.590 --> 00:18:29.049
claimants who’ve accused the mining company
of, among other things, beating, stabbing,

00:18:29.049 --> 00:18:31.519
and detaining them during their time as miners.

00:18:31.519 --> 00:18:35.950
While the settlement makes “no admission
of liability,” Petra has vowed to fund community

00:18:35.950 --> 00:18:38.309
projects and prevent future incidents.

00:18:38.309 --> 00:18:43.900
In the Ivory Coast, stamping out illegal cocoa
grow operations has proved next to impossible.

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:47.820
But that could change with the adoption of
the most recent European Commission plan to

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:49.940
curb global deforestation.

00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:54.460
If implemented, this plan would require companies
importing cocoa products into the EU to provide

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:58.579
GPS data on the product's origin to ensure
it wasn’t illegally harvested.

00:18:58.579 --> 00:19:02.820
For the Ivory Coast, spreading the responsibility
of halting illegal cocoa production will help

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:06.809
protect the meager 15% of its biodiverse forest
still standing.

00:19:06.809 --> 00:19:11.639
It’s been 15 years since Shell’s Nigerian
subsidiary’s pipelines poisoned the farmlands

00:19:11.639 --> 00:19:16.350
of the villages of Goi and Oruma, and 13 years
since the case went to court.

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:21.190
But this year, a Dutch court finally ruled
that Royal Dutch Shell and its Nigerian subsidiary

00:19:21.190 --> 00:19:25.950
must compensate the villages for a still undecided
amount and install warning systems along its

00:19:25.950 --> 00:19:28.059
pipelines to curb the next disaster.

00:19:28.059 --> 00:19:32.929
In Rwanda, the crackdown on dissidents under
President Paul Kagame’s government has extended

00:19:32.929 --> 00:19:34.220
to YouTube creators.

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:38.580
Dieudonne Niyonsenga, whose channel, Ishema
TV covers topics such as human rights abuses

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:42.960
and has over 15 million views, was sentenced
to seven years in prison after being found

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:47.299
guilty of forgery, humiliation of state officials,
and two other charges.

00:19:47.299 --> 00:19:51.350
He was the second Rwandan YouTube critic to
be arrested in a country where freedom of

00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:54.679
expression is becoming increasingly costly.

00:19:54.679 --> 00:19:59.620
Expressing dissent with Uganda’s 900 mile,
1,500 kilometer oil pipeline project is becoming

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:00.899
costly, too.

00:20:00.899 --> 00:20:05.299
Citing lack of compensation for land and a
host of environmental dangers, local activists

00:20:05.299 --> 00:20:09.370
and international NGOs are raising concerns
over the project that begins in Murchison

00:20:09.370 --> 00:20:11.840
National Park and borders Lake Victoria.

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:15.649
It seems they’re fighting an uphill battle
against the project, which is backed by Tanzania,

00:20:15.649 --> 00:20:20.830
Uganda, and companies from France and China,
as Uganda has arrested six protestors––a

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:23.409
move some see as a scare tactic.

00:20:23.409 --> 00:20:26.570
Elsewhere on the continent, people’s voices
are getting through.

00:20:26.570 --> 00:20:31.980
In Benin, a law passed that drastically expands
access to abortion and largely puts the decision

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:33.409
in women’s hands.

00:20:33.409 --> 00:20:38.480
What used to be legal only in cases of life-threatening
pregnancies, rape, and incestuous relationships,

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:43.280
is now allowed on account of a wide range
of reasons within the first 12 weeks of pregnancy.

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:47.470
Globally, this move has been hailed by women’s
rights activists, and regionally, this makes

00:20:47.470 --> 00:20:50.590
Benin the most progressive country on the
issue in West Africa.

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:55.749
In a country that’s 90% Sahara, with only
one permanent river, water’s hard to come

00:20:55.749 --> 00:20:56.749
by.

00:20:56.749 --> 00:21:00.809
Facing shortages and inconsistent supply,
Mauritania recently launched a fundraising

00:21:00.809 --> 00:21:06.690
effort to secure $317 million for a project
that will provide water to half a million

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:08.820
in the south of the arid country.

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:12.480
Counting on several financiers from across
the Arab world, the project will pipe water

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:16.850
to urban and rural users in a bid to minimize
the gap between the two and help to realize

00:21:16.850 --> 00:21:18.840
the region’s agricultural potential.

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:22.999
From a country with limited water, to one
with little land, it’s been a momentous

00:21:22.999 --> 00:21:27.490
year for sports fans on the island of Comoros,
as the nation of under a million was ground

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:30.929
zero for a history-making run by the national
soccer team.

00:21:30.929 --> 00:21:35.269
Minnows on the continental level, Comoros
held World Cup qualifiers Egypt to a draw

00:21:35.269 --> 00:21:39.679
and went unbeaten during the African Cup League
of Nations qualifying stage—a Cinderella

00:21:39.679 --> 00:21:43.070
run that will see the nation make the cup
tournament for the first time.

00:21:43.070 --> 00:21:47.200
Not bad for a team that didn’t even exist
until 2006.

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:50.789
As teamseas-ers, we don’t condone tossing
trash into the ocean.

00:21:50.789 --> 00:21:53.620
But sometimes such littering lends itself
to a story.

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:57.950
Picking up plastic along the beaches of a
Papua New Guinean Island, Steven Amos stumbled

00:21:57.950 --> 00:22:03.049
across a message in a bottle that had traveled
1,500 miles, or 2,400 kilometers, after being

00:22:03.049 --> 00:22:05.549
tossed overboard by an American sailor two
years prior.

00:22:05.549 --> 00:22:09.759
In a nation struggling with major plastic
pollution, perhaps Amos smiled not only at

00:22:09.759 --> 00:22:13.929
the friendly note, but also at the fact that
it was contained in a nice, reusable glass

00:22:13.929 --> 00:22:15.200
bottle.

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:18.299
How young is too young to charge someone with
a crime?

00:22:18.299 --> 00:22:22.289
Pakistan faced down this very question when
they charged an 8-year-old of blasphemy when

00:22:22.289 --> 00:22:24.990
the Hindu child peed in an Islamic school
library.

00:22:24.990 --> 00:22:29.690
In the incident’s wake, police charged the
boy while, in reaction to the perceived disrespect,

00:22:29.690 --> 00:22:31.899
a mob vandalized a Hindu temple.

00:22:31.899 --> 00:22:36.109
Eventually though, the state stepped in and
punished the cops who arrested the child,

00:22:36.109 --> 00:22:40.019
dropped all charges, and organized payment
for the temple’s repairs.

00:22:40.019 --> 00:22:43.879
Charges that are sticking, however, were laid
against two transgender women who were simply

00:22:43.879 --> 00:22:47.650
having dinner in Cameroon when they were arrested
for alleged homosexuality.

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:52.470
Later, they were sentenced to five years in
jail, which some say is a death sentence in

00:22:52.470 --> 00:22:54.540
the male-dominated prison system.

00:22:54.540 --> 00:22:58.179
The arrests and high-profile nature of the
case—as one of the women is an activist

00:22:58.179 --> 00:23:02.769
for trans rights—exemplifies the anti-LGBTQ
sentiments and laws that are still common

00:23:02.769 --> 00:23:07.519
in African countries, with at least 30 of
them criminalizing homosexuality.

00:23:07.519 --> 00:23:11.389
Technology seems to be a storyline, and this
is clearly the case for ISIS-affiliated women

00:23:11.389 --> 00:23:15.960
held at the Al-Hawl refugee camp in Syria,
where they are marrying men they meet online

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:20.160
in order to receive income and the slim chance
of eventual repatriation.

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:24.960
The camp, which is home to nearly 60,000 women
and children, is teeming with squalor and

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:26.220
extremist ideas.

00:23:26.220 --> 00:23:30.370
Yet, jihadist men reportedly see it as a badge
of honor to care for the women there through

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:35.200
these virtual marriages, even though they
rarely move beyond anything but digital.

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:39.500
For something much more tangible, a Solomon
Islands man digging in his backyard to install

00:23:39.500 --> 00:23:43.960
a septic system struck something hard, but
this was the opposite of treasure.

00:23:43.960 --> 00:23:49.210
Instead, he discovered more than 100 unexploded
World War II bombs, which came as a real surprise

00:23:49.210 --> 00:23:53.630
and a tragic reminder of the devastation the
Pacific Islands suffered during that era,

00:23:53.630 --> 00:23:57.769
when thousands of bombs were dropped and munitions
stores, like this one, were common.

00:23:57.769 --> 00:24:02.789
The ordinances were successfully, and thankfully,
removed by a specialized team.

00:24:02.789 --> 00:24:07.789
In the opposite of underground, Zimbabwe plans
to launch its first satellite, the ZIMSAT-1,

00:24:07.789 --> 00:24:08.789
in February.

00:24:08.789 --> 00:24:12.830
It will be used to help organizations and
officials collect data that relates to ground

00:24:12.830 --> 00:24:16.739
cover and drought, as well as transmit signals
for amateur radio stations.

00:24:16.739 --> 00:24:20.950
Zimbabwe’s space program has grown quickly
in just three years since being established,

00:24:20.950 --> 00:24:24.710
and follows on the heels of other African
countries extending upward as well.

00:24:24.710 --> 00:24:28.990
Though many countries focus on the pressing
humanitarian needs first, the continent’s

00:24:28.990 --> 00:24:34.700
satellite fleet now stands at 44 as they increasingly
recognize the importance of strategic space

00:24:34.700 --> 00:24:35.700
investment.

00:24:35.700 --> 00:24:40.239
Break out those dancing shoes, because the
rumba may officially be a part of worldwide

00:24:40.239 --> 00:24:42.629
intangible cultural heritage.

00:24:42.629 --> 00:24:46.820
The Republic of the Congo and the Democratic
Republic of the Congo applied to UNESCO for

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:51.919
the official designation, which would protect
the dance’s history and cement its importance.

00:24:51.919 --> 00:24:56.610
The rumba was born in 19th-century Cuba, combining
the drumming of enslaved Africans with the

00:24:56.610 --> 00:24:58.340
melodies of Spanish colonizers.

00:24:58.340 --> 00:25:02.429
But it’s not just of historical importance,
because the rumba has modern-day relevance

00:25:02.429 --> 00:25:06.899
too, including the 1960’s “Independence
Cha Cha,” a version of the rumba that celebrated

00:25:06.899 --> 00:25:08.760
the freedom of Belgian Congo.

00:25:08.760 --> 00:25:13.299
In the same region, downstream effects from
an Angolan diamond mine were catastrophically

00:25:13.299 --> 00:25:18.249
felt in a toxic spill that sickened more than
1 million people, killed 12, and decimated

00:25:18.249 --> 00:25:19.889
fish and wildlife.

00:25:19.889 --> 00:25:23.919
Waste from the largest diamond mine in Angola
leaked into the Kasai River, which eventually

00:25:23.919 --> 00:25:27.919
reached the Congo river, where large amounts
of heavy metal were detected.

00:25:27.919 --> 00:25:31.990
The mine, which is partially owned by a Russian
company, acknowledged there had been a leak,

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:36.289
but said that it was just sand and mud, and
of course, they claimed no responsibility

00:25:36.289 --> 00:25:37.289
for the disaster.

00:25:37.289 --> 00:25:41.210
There’s no accountability in this story
either, but there is a bit of good news from

00:25:41.210 --> 00:25:45.440
a country that’s increasingly cracking down
on the freedom of the press: Myanmar released

00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:50.009
American journalist Danny Fenster from prison
just days after he was sentenced to 11 years

00:25:50.009 --> 00:25:53.830
for charges that included incitement of violence
and visa violations.

00:25:53.830 --> 00:25:57.919
The military in Myanmar has clamped down on
the media since seizing power in February,

00:25:57.919 --> 00:26:01.549
stripping publications of their licenses and
banning outlets from broadcasting.

00:26:01.549 --> 00:26:06.690
Fenster, however, was the first foreign journalist
to be convicted for charges he says were without

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:12.770
warrant, and thanks to negotiations by a former
US ambassador to the UN, now he’ll go free.

00:26:12.770 --> 00:26:16.809
Imprisonment in an authoritarian country is
one person’s nightmare, while 10 million

00:26:16.809 --> 00:26:20.990
fruit bats descending on a condensed swamp
area might sound like another’s.

00:26:20.990 --> 00:26:24.529
But for conservationists in Zambia, it’s
essential.

00:26:24.529 --> 00:26:28.779
The bats are key to African ecology because
they eat fruits and berries and distribute

00:26:28.779 --> 00:26:31.090
the seeds during their continental migration.

00:26:31.090 --> 00:26:36.090
Now these environmentalists are fighting a
proposal for a 17,500-acre, or 7,000-hectare,

00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:40.730
wheat, soy, and corn farm that would be built
on the national park’s borders and threaten

00:26:40.730 --> 00:26:44.340
the bat’s habitat, and existence, altogether.

00:26:44.340 --> 00:26:49.409
In another story about West African instability,
Guinea junta leader Mamady Doumbouya was sworn

00:26:49.409 --> 00:26:53.899
in after a successful September coup, marking
the fourth coup in the region in the past

00:26:53.899 --> 00:26:54.899
year.

00:26:54.899 --> 00:26:59.059
Doumboya’s party overthrew former president
Alpha Conde for what they said was continued

00:26:59.059 --> 00:27:03.409
corruption, but political upheaval continues
to mire the area, and now a regional economic

00:27:03.409 --> 00:27:08.730
council has placed economic sanctions and
travel restrictions on the Guinean junta.

00:27:08.730 --> 00:27:12.629
Sometimes governmental overreach isn’t always
so unpleasant, as is the case with a defanged

00:27:12.629 --> 00:27:14.340
and declawed lion named Hima.

00:27:14.340 --> 00:27:18.600
He was reunited with his owner after the Cambodian
prime minister got involved.

00:27:18.600 --> 00:27:22.980
Initially, Hima was seized after TikTok videos
of him living casually in a high-end Phnom

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:26.159
Penh neighborhood—something the government
deemed inappropriate—went viral.

00:27:26.159 --> 00:27:31.200
But additional posts showed a clear bond between
the two after their separation, and following

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:36.950
public calls for reunification, the prime
minister announced the two would be rejoined.

00:27:36.950 --> 00:27:41.909
Speaking of lions, Kenya is known as a wildlife
wonderland, but just how many creatures crawl

00:27:41.909 --> 00:27:47.390
there was an unknown number until the country
completed its first wildlife census this year.

00:27:47.390 --> 00:27:52.399
Using planes, drones, helicopters, boats,
and 4x4s, researchers tallied the populations

00:27:52.399 --> 00:27:57.190
of more than 30 species across nearly 60%
of the country to establish a baseline for

00:27:57.190 --> 00:28:00.220
one of the world’s most unique ecological
places.

00:28:00.220 --> 00:28:05.249
Using estimates from the past, the numbers
of elephants, some 36,280, seem to be going

00:28:05.249 --> 00:28:11.780
up, but black rhinos, just 897, have fallen
to critically endangered levels.

00:28:11.780 --> 00:28:16.399
Kenya counts wildlife, and Nepal counts how
many people reach the top of Mount Everest.

00:28:16.399 --> 00:28:20.799
Now, they’re unhappy with three Indian climbers
who allegedly falsified their summit of the

00:28:20.799 --> 00:28:23.309
world’s highest peak in 2016.

00:28:23.309 --> 00:28:27.070
This year, the Nepalese government penalized
them by banning them for six years, essentially

00:28:27.070 --> 00:28:30.389
preventing them from actually trying to get
to the top another time.

00:28:30.389 --> 00:28:34.370
The Nepalese ministry verifies climbs through
photographic evidence, and while these three

00:28:34.370 --> 00:28:38.710
had the photos, presumably photoshopped, they
reportedly didn’t have enough oxygen to

00:28:38.710 --> 00:28:41.259
have feasibly completed the trip.

00:28:41.259 --> 00:28:45.929
Back at sea level, impoverished East Timor
is at an interesting economic crossroads,

00:28:45.929 --> 00:28:50.629
exploring eco-tourism, offshore drilling,
and now, carbon capture storage as a way to

00:28:50.629 --> 00:28:52.389
boost the country’s revenue.

00:28:52.389 --> 00:28:57.230
Australia has proposed capturing its own carbon
waste and paying to store it in East Timor’s

00:28:57.230 --> 00:29:01.559
nearly depleted Bayu-Undan oil field, which
is expected to run out in the next couple

00:29:01.559 --> 00:29:02.750
of years.

00:29:02.750 --> 00:29:07.409
Proponents see this as a creative environmental
solution, and critics say it’s simply shifting

00:29:07.409 --> 00:29:11.360
climate change responsibility from a rich
country to a poor one.

00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:15.970
Nearby Vanuatu is a remote Pacific island
with just 300,000 residents.

00:29:15.970 --> 00:29:20.879
So why did some 2,000 politicians, business
owners, and criminals get citizenship there

00:29:20.879 --> 00:29:21.879
recently?

00:29:21.879 --> 00:29:24.509
Because for $130,000, they can.

00:29:24.509 --> 00:29:29.331
The citizenship-by-investment program is legal,
and gives these new residents visa-free access

00:29:29.331 --> 00:29:31.659
to the UK and European Union.

00:29:31.659 --> 00:29:35.820
But, some worry the increased interest in
this island—where the passport program accounted

00:29:35.820 --> 00:29:41.259
for nearly 42% of government revenue in 2020—might
be fertile ground for money laundering and

00:29:41.259 --> 00:29:42.509
international mischief.

00:29:42.509 --> 00:29:47.991
In a move that signals the increasing geopolitical
importance of Africa, Twitter hired its first

00:29:47.991 --> 00:29:52.220
employees on the continent in the country
of Ghana, where freedom of speech protections

00:29:52.220 --> 00:29:55.370
and online freedoms made it an ideal location.

00:29:55.370 --> 00:29:59.149
The social media company announced it would
fill 11 positions, with plans to officially

00:29:59.149 --> 00:30:04.149
open an office there once pandemic restrictions
and work-from-home orders are relaxed.

00:30:04.149 --> 00:30:09.369
For more than 35 years, Eswatini’s King
Mswati III has ruled as absolute monarch and

00:30:09.369 --> 00:30:12.370
flaunted a lavish, luxurious lifestyle.

00:30:12.370 --> 00:30:13.659
The problem?

00:30:13.659 --> 00:30:18.230
Sixty percent of the country’s 1.1 million
residents live in poverty and they’re over

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:19.460
his opulence.

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:23.840
For months, residents have participated in
pro-democracy protests that have left scores

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:26.440
dead and many more wounded.

00:30:26.440 --> 00:30:30.919
Neighboring African countries, including South
Africa, upon which Eswatini heavily relies,

00:30:30.919 --> 00:30:34.980
are stepping in to help navigate the movement,
which many say won’t be going away until

00:30:34.980 --> 00:30:37.429
there’s a brighter future for this developing
nation.

00:30:37.429 --> 00:30:42.059
A plot to smuggle 55 million methamphetamine
tablets and more than 1.5 tons of crystal

00:30:42.059 --> 00:30:46.899
meth disguised in beer crates was thwarted
by police in Laos, resulting in the largest

00:30:46.899 --> 00:30:48.679
single drug seizure in Asia.

00:30:48.679 --> 00:30:52.509
They were discovered in a truck in an area
called the “Golden Triangle”––the

00:30:52.509 --> 00:30:57.359
meeting point of Myanmar, Laos, and Thailand,
which is notorious as a hotspot for drug production

00:30:57.359 --> 00:30:58.470
and smuggling.

00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:02.590
With recent unrest in Myanmar’s Shan region
and Chinese crackdown on the trade, smugglers

00:31:02.590 --> 00:31:07.149
have looked to alternative routes––in
this case, without much success.

00:31:07.149 --> 00:31:12.090
In another superlative, Pacific Islander Nicole
Yamase became the first citizen from The Federated

00:31:12.090 --> 00:31:16.799
States of Micronesia to reach Challenger Deep––the
deepest part of the Mariana Trench, which

00:31:16.799 --> 00:31:19.340
lies within the territorial waters of the
country.

00:31:19.340 --> 00:31:24.529
Her completion of the 10-hour journey, which
took her to 36,000 feet, or 11,000 meters,

00:31:24.529 --> 00:31:30.010
below the ocean’s surface, also makes her
only the third woman, and at 29, the second-youngest,

00:31:30.010 --> 00:31:31.029
to ever do it.

00:31:31.029 --> 00:31:35.609
She's a PhD student studying climate change’s
effects on microalgae, and going to the world’s

00:31:35.609 --> 00:31:38.609
most remote places for research is all in
a days’ work for her.

00:31:38.609 --> 00:31:43.369
On the topic of climate change, in an effort
to reduce its dependence on fossil fuels,

00:31:43.369 --> 00:31:48.840
Sao Tome and Principe made an innovative announcement
involving deepwater platforms—but not the

00:31:48.840 --> 00:31:49.840
oil kind.

00:31:49.840 --> 00:31:54.529
Instead, they’re engaging in a public-private
partnership to debut Ocean Thermal Energy

00:31:54.529 --> 00:31:58.509
Conversion technology––the first commercial
endeavor of its kind.

00:31:58.509 --> 00:32:02.649
The floating platforms use the difference
between cold deep ocean water and warm surface

00:32:02.649 --> 00:32:06.340
temperatures to create green energy out of
the blue.

00:32:06.340 --> 00:32:10.830
Another island nation concerned for its future
is Kiribati, where six years ago, the government

00:32:10.830 --> 00:32:15.590
starred in its own version of “House Hunters”
and purchased some 8.5 square miles, or 22

00:32:15.590 --> 00:32:19.080
square kilometers, of land on Fiji to make
sure its potentially displaced residents had

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:21.499
somewhere to go as sea levels rise.

00:32:21.499 --> 00:32:25.330
To use that land in the meantime, Kiribati
enlisted the help of omnipresent China to

00:32:25.330 --> 00:32:29.169
build farms that would provide fresh produce
and meat to the island nation, which currently

00:32:29.169 --> 00:32:30.890
imports all of its food.

00:32:30.890 --> 00:32:35.220
Some say this is another land grab by China,
while others see it as a sustainability measure

00:32:35.220 --> 00:32:36.220
for Kiribati.

00:32:36.220 --> 00:32:40.840
Both of these takes miss what might be the
most interesting part of this story: an actual

00:32:40.840 --> 00:32:44.429
overseas climate refuge in the first place.

00:32:44.429 --> 00:32:49.070
Garment workers in Bangladesh are more protected
after an agreement was signed by some 200

00:32:49.070 --> 00:32:52.519
international fashion companies that includes
legally binding safety commitments, independent

00:32:52.519 --> 00:32:57.020
inspections at the factories, and contributions
for safety training and factory improvements.

00:32:57.020 --> 00:33:01.559
The accord replaces one that was created after
a devastating factory fire that killed 1,100

00:33:01.559 --> 00:33:04.070
people in 2013 and was set to expire.

00:33:04.070 --> 00:33:07.830
Human rights workers praised the move, which
means protections will continue into the future,

00:33:07.830 --> 00:33:12.239
and keep Bangladesh as one of the top garment
producers in the world.

00:33:12.239 --> 00:33:16.379
That pandemic coffee habit may be helping
boost Honduras’ economy, according to recent

00:33:16.379 --> 00:33:17.740
economic reports.

00:33:17.740 --> 00:33:23.399
GDP growth for 2021 was expected to be between
2% and 5%, but new projections show almost

00:33:23.399 --> 00:33:27.179
double that, with the country’s economy
predicted to grow by almost 9%.

00:33:27.179 --> 00:33:31.210
This is thanks to increased activity in retail
and manufacturing, and, because of supply-chain

00:33:31.210 --> 00:33:35.950
issues, higher prices for coffee, which is
one of the country’s primary exports.

00:33:35.950 --> 00:33:39.880
If two’s a party and three’s a crowd,
then India is taking note.

00:33:39.880 --> 00:33:43.850
Some states, including the country’s most
populous, Uttar Pradesh, which has 240 million

00:33:43.850 --> 00:33:48.159
people and would be the world’s fifth most
populous country by itself, introduced a China-like

00:33:48.159 --> 00:33:51.830
two-child policy and sterilization measures
to control population growth.

00:33:51.830 --> 00:33:55.980
Though it still has to be adopted, the proposal
would deny state benefits, subsidies, and

00:33:55.980 --> 00:34:00.369
jobs to anyone who has more than two children,
and dangles the carrot of incentives for anyone

00:34:00.369 --> 00:34:01.940
who gets sterilized.

00:34:01.940 --> 00:34:05.450
Twelve states have actually adopted similar
measures, all in an effort to limit growth

00:34:05.450 --> 00:34:08.350
in a country that’s already the second most
populous in the world.

00:34:08.350 --> 00:34:14.039
In the early 1900s, Namibia was known as German
South West Africa, and Germany is still dealing

00:34:14.039 --> 00:34:18.260
with its dark history there, which included
troops that killed, tortured, and forced tens

00:34:18.260 --> 00:34:21.210
of thousands of native peoples into the desert
to starve.

00:34:21.210 --> 00:34:26.319
This year, then-Chancellor Angela Merkel reached
a deal that will distribute $1.1 billion Euros

00:34:26.319 --> 00:34:29.679
over 30 years to benefit aid programs in the
African country.

00:34:29.679 --> 00:34:33.510
But, she stopped short of calling the funding
“reparations,” and instead said it was

00:34:33.510 --> 00:34:38.389
“reconciliation” money that would help
attempt to “heal the wounds” of the past.

00:34:38.389 --> 00:34:42.609
China continues to expand its presence around
the globe, and in Bhutan this hasn’t been

00:34:42.609 --> 00:34:43.609
subtle.

00:34:43.609 --> 00:34:47.010
In the past year, satellite imagery shows
that multiple new villages have sprung up

00:34:47.010 --> 00:34:51.820
in a 60-square-mile, or 150-square kilometer,
Bhutanese area that’s been a disputed site

00:34:51.820 --> 00:34:55.500
before, when India and China went to head
about road construction there a handful of

00:34:55.500 --> 00:34:56.579
years ago.

00:34:56.579 --> 00:34:59.920
Because of aligned interests when it comes
to Chinese overreach, India has supported

00:34:59.920 --> 00:35:02.780
Bhutan’s claims to the contested territory.

00:35:02.780 --> 00:35:06.970
Across the world, the situation in Nicaragua
right now is unequivocally bad, and it wasn’t

00:35:06.970 --> 00:35:11.540
much better in early 2021 when President Daniel
Ortega opted to shoot for the moon, even though

00:35:11.540 --> 00:35:14.220
the nation is mired in social and economic
misery.

00:35:14.220 --> 00:35:17.960
That’s when he established the National
Ministry for Extraterrestrial Space Affairs,

00:35:17.960 --> 00:35:22.740
The Moon and Other Celestial Bodies, which
people are calling a vanity project.

00:35:22.740 --> 00:35:26.180
Its goal is to develop a space agency that
will reach the farthest points in the universe,

00:35:26.180 --> 00:35:29.539
but it quite literally hasn’t gotten off
the ground.

00:35:29.539 --> 00:35:34.809
When one thinks of palm oil plantations, Malaysia
and Indonesia usually come to mind.

00:35:34.809 --> 00:35:38.900
But Guatemala’s emerging market makes it
the world’s sixth-largest producer of palm

00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:42.890
oil, however that success, of course, comes
at a cost.

00:35:42.890 --> 00:35:47.099
More production has led to companies grabbing
indigenous lands at an increasing rate.

00:35:47.099 --> 00:35:51.849
In response, some of Guatemala’s indigenous
population––almost 40% of the country’s

00:35:51.849 --> 00:35:56.589
17 million residents––are resisting by
forcefully moving back onto the land.

00:35:56.589 --> 00:36:01.480
Native baby sea turtle numbers on the 10-island
nation of Cape Verde are booming, in a rabbit-like

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:02.480
way.

00:36:02.480 --> 00:36:07.089
The good news is that nest numbers last year
were up to 200,000, which is 20 times the

00:36:07.089 --> 00:36:08.400
estimates from 2015.

00:36:08.400 --> 00:36:12.589
The bad news is that sea turtles’ gender
is determined by the temperature of the sand

00:36:12.589 --> 00:36:18.440
where the eggs are laid, and, well, the globally-warmed
beach means that about 84% of baby sea turtles

00:36:18.440 --> 00:36:22.559
hatchlings are now female, which unfortunately
still keeps the species on the endangered

00:36:22.559 --> 00:36:23.559
list.

00:36:23.559 --> 00:36:27.150
When it comes to finding fodder for Central
Asian geopolitical cocktail talk, here’s

00:36:27.150 --> 00:36:30.990
a fun fact: Tajikistan’s primary export
is migrant labor.

00:36:30.990 --> 00:36:36.510
By September 2021, more than 1.6 million Tajiks
left the country to work in Russia.

00:36:36.510 --> 00:36:40.480
These migrant workers then send billions of
dollars in remittances back home.

00:36:40.480 --> 00:36:45.640
In 2019, actually, this figure accounted for
more than one-quarter of Tajikistan’s GDP,

00:36:45.640 --> 00:36:49.640
making the ex-Soviet country still very dependent
on Russia.

00:36:49.640 --> 00:36:53.180
Anyone who invested early in Bitcoin should
head straight to El Salvador where they can

00:36:53.180 --> 00:36:57.819
make it crypto-rain now that the country became
the first in the world to adopt the currency

00:36:57.819 --> 00:36:59.050
as legal tender.

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:03.349
Though some, including major lender the International
Monetary Fund, are skeptical because of the

00:37:03.349 --> 00:37:07.460
volatile and risky nature of cryptocurrencies,
others are optimistic, such as Salvodorians

00:37:07.460 --> 00:37:11.540
who can dip into their Chivo wallet app and
find $30 of Bitcoin waiting for them.

00:37:11.540 --> 00:37:16.060
In the ongoing fight against ISIS, officials
in Iraq declared a moderate victory with the

00:37:16.060 --> 00:37:19.390
capture of the Islamic State’s finance minister,
Sami Jassem al-Ajuz.

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:20.390
The kicker?

00:37:20.390 --> 00:37:21.390
He’s alive.

00:37:21.390 --> 00:37:24.589
The arrest could provide Iraq and international
allies with a treasure trove of intelligence

00:37:24.589 --> 00:37:28.490
about the terrorist organization that, although
has retreated from daily headlines in recent

00:37:28.490 --> 00:37:31.990
years, maintains quite active in sleeper cells
throughout the Middle East.

00:37:31.990 --> 00:37:35.990
Two residents in Guyana are not staying quiet
about protecting the planet, something they

00:37:35.990 --> 00:37:38.039
argue is a fundamental right to humanity’s
sustainability.

00:37:38.039 --> 00:37:43.000
The pair is suing their country for having
approved oil exploration licenses, saying

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:46.240
it violates the government’s legal duty
to protect the right of future generations

00:37:46.240 --> 00:37:47.720
to a healthy environment.

00:37:47.720 --> 00:37:51.720
This David-versus-Goliath fight pits them
against ExxonMobil, which is eyeing a potential

00:37:51.720 --> 00:37:55.809
8 billion gallons of oil in offshore drilling,
which would make it the company’s largest

00:37:55.809 --> 00:37:57.530
extraction site in the world.

00:37:57.530 --> 00:38:02.180
There’s nothing like the discovery of 1.3
million-year-old stone tools to make this

00:38:02.180 --> 00:38:05.410
point in time seem, well, short.

00:38:05.410 --> 00:38:08.890
Archeologists in Morocco recently discovered
the artifacts in a site associated with human

00:38:08.890 --> 00:38:13.539
ancestor homo erectus, almost doubling the
previous start date for the dawn of the stone-tool

00:38:13.539 --> 00:38:15.020
era in north Africa.

00:38:15.020 --> 00:38:19.030
What’s mind-boggling is that these tools
aren’t even close to the oldest traces of

00:38:19.030 --> 00:38:23.450
human ancestors, which date all the way back
to more than 4 million years ago.

00:38:23.450 --> 00:38:28.190
In Kyrgyzstan, Central Asia’s only democracy,
just over one-third of the population turned

00:38:28.190 --> 00:38:33.210
out in a snap election that put populist politician
and convicted kidnapper Sadyr Japarov into

00:38:33.210 --> 00:38:34.210
power.

00:38:34.210 --> 00:38:37.680
The former Soviet country sits between China,
which has invested in its infrastructure,

00:38:37.680 --> 00:38:41.400
and Russia, to which it still has significant
loyalties, including from its newly elected

00:38:41.400 --> 00:38:45.280
president who has vowed to remain allied with
both neighbors—something that will be tough

00:38:45.280 --> 00:38:49.059
to balance amid his own country’s partially
self-inflicted political turmoil.

00:38:49.059 --> 00:38:52.980
There’s some positive news about elephants
coming out of Gabon, where new research shows

00:38:52.980 --> 00:38:57.030
the population of forest elephants—a cousin
to the savanna-dwelling pachyderms—seem

00:38:57.030 --> 00:38:59.210
to be doing better than expected.

00:38:59.210 --> 00:39:02.730
Previous estimates reported there were about
50,000 left in the Central African country,

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:07.380
but a recent study shows that number to be
closer to 95,000, thanks to strict anti-poaching

00:39:07.380 --> 00:39:10.180
regulations and land conservation measures.

00:39:10.180 --> 00:39:14.010
Athleisure-wearing, latte-drinking urbanites
might need an extra downward dog and shot

00:39:14.010 --> 00:39:17.641
of espresso to deal with their supply-chain
woes this year—especially with products

00:39:17.641 --> 00:39:21.840
like Lululemon and coffee coming from Vietnam,
where a combination of lockdowns and factory

00:39:21.840 --> 00:39:25.800
conditions has prompted millions of workers
to leave cities and return to their countryside

00:39:25.800 --> 00:39:26.800
homes.

00:39:26.800 --> 00:39:30.621
Manufacturers are having a tough time luring
them back, and that was bad news for a supply

00:39:30.621 --> 00:39:32.330
chain already in crisis.

00:39:32.330 --> 00:39:36.910
An Arizona man who conducted a years-long
human trafficking ring selling babies from

00:39:36.910 --> 00:39:40.430
the Marshall Island to American parents was
sentenced to six years in prison and forced

00:39:40.430 --> 00:39:43.130
to pay hundreds of thousands in restitution
charges.

00:39:43.130 --> 00:39:47.380
The man would pay around $10,000 for pregnant
Marshallese women to come to the United States

00:39:47.380 --> 00:39:51.430
and then arrange for their newborns to be
adopted by couples for $40,000.

00:39:51.430 --> 00:39:55.450
Officials estimate some 70 adoptions took
place this way, taking advantage of the “compact

00:39:55.450 --> 00:39:59.200
of free association,” which allowed them
to travel or move to the US without restriction.

00:39:59.200 --> 00:40:05.020
On the subject of pregnancy, Polish researchers
discovered a first: an Egyptian mummy with

00:40:05.020 --> 00:40:06.020
child.

00:40:06.020 --> 00:40:09.589
Originally they thought the mummy mommy was
a male priest, but further investigation uncovered

00:40:09.589 --> 00:40:14.490
a small femur bone thought to belong to a
fetus about 25 to 30 weeks old.

00:40:14.490 --> 00:40:17.990
Scientists called the find unique, and important
to Egyptology, in which little research has

00:40:17.990 --> 00:40:19.590
been done on females.

00:40:19.590 --> 00:40:24.230
In not-so-sweet news, Ben &amp; Jerry’s announced
it would stop selling its ice cream in occupied

00:40:24.230 --> 00:40:29.170
Palestinian territories, making a very public
corporate statement against the Israeli settlements

00:40:29.170 --> 00:40:32.140
there, which are not recognized by international
law.

00:40:32.140 --> 00:40:35.990
Israeli officials called the decision a “capitulation
to antisemitism.”

00:40:35.990 --> 00:40:39.859
The brand, however, will still be available
throughout the internationally recognized

00:40:39.859 --> 00:40:43.110
territory of Israel, which sort of makes this
ban “half-baked.”

00:40:43.110 --> 00:40:47.410
It’s banned for poachers to remove succulents
from the desert in South Africa, but that

00:40:47.410 --> 00:40:51.230
hasn’t stopped it from being an explosive
problem in a country that’s home to one-third

00:40:51.230 --> 00:40:52.880
of the world’s succulent species.

00:40:52.880 --> 00:40:57.350
Specifically, people are harvesting Conophytum,
which is a genus of flowering plants that

00:40:57.350 --> 00:41:01.109
consists of over 100 species, many of which
are on the endangered list.

00:41:01.109 --> 00:41:04.860
They’re then sold to hot markets including
Korea and China, where the plants that were

00:41:04.860 --> 00:41:08.990
once considered low-class have become popular
for being low-maintenance.

00:41:08.990 --> 00:41:12.549
Across the pond, Venezuelans have been fleeing
their country in one of the world’s largest

00:41:12.549 --> 00:41:16.130
human migrations, and one-third of them head
to neighboring Colombia.

00:41:16.130 --> 00:41:19.820
Recently, Colombia announced its decision
to provide temporary legal status to more

00:41:19.820 --> 00:41:23.460
than 1.7 million migrants who had fled there
in recent years.

00:41:23.460 --> 00:41:26.700
That means the migrants can work legally for
up to 10 years.

00:41:26.700 --> 00:41:30.560
A collapsed economy has created a humanitarian
crisis, and refugees in search of a better

00:41:30.560 --> 00:41:34.579
life may find that with Colombia’s legal
acceptance of them.

00:41:34.579 --> 00:41:39.440
Last year the president of Turkmenistan erected
a gilded statue of a beloved breed of dog,

00:41:39.440 --> 00:41:40.440
the Alabai.

00:41:40.440 --> 00:41:45.980
This year, he took it a step further and announced
a national holiday honoring the large, shepherd-like

00:41:45.980 --> 00:41:46.980
canine.

00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:51.359
The paw-liday included a contest for the country’s
best dog and a medal given to its top border

00:41:51.359 --> 00:41:52.380
guard dog.

00:41:52.380 --> 00:41:56.770
Alabai now shares the special day with another
of Turkmenistan’s celebrated: the Akhal-Teke

00:41:56.770 --> 00:41:57.840
horse breed.

00:41:57.840 --> 00:42:02.180
Both are considered important parts of the
national heritage, where herding is a traditional

00:42:02.180 --> 00:42:03.180
pastime.

00:42:03.180 --> 00:42:07.130
Traditionally, the transfer of power is supposed
to be peaceful and seamless.

00:42:07.130 --> 00:42:09.109
In Samoa, it wasn’t.

00:42:09.109 --> 00:42:13.410
Fiame Naomi Mata’afa finally became the
country’s prime minister in July, following

00:42:13.410 --> 00:42:19.280
several months of constitutional crisis during
which her predecessor, Tuilaepa Sailele Malielegaoi,

00:42:19.280 --> 00:42:20.960
refused to acknowledge her victory.

00:42:20.960 --> 00:42:24.619
The former leader had led the country for
more than two decades, and would not step

00:42:24.619 --> 00:42:25.619
down.

00:42:25.619 --> 00:42:29.510
After a series of legal battles and appeals,
Mata’afa finally assumed control and is

00:42:29.510 --> 00:42:33.160
making history doing it as Samoa’s first
female prime minister.

00:42:33.160 --> 00:42:38.250
In Belize, officials are both making and saving
history–ecologically, that is.

00:42:38.250 --> 00:42:42.960
This year a coalition of conservation organizations
banded together to save one of the world’s

00:42:42.960 --> 00:42:48.000
last pristine rainforests, preserving 367
square miles, or 950 square kilometers of

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:49.710
land from deforestation.

00:42:49.710 --> 00:42:54.950
This area is part of the larger, which crosses
Mexico, Belize, and Guatemala, and means a

00:42:54.950 --> 00:42:59.880
biodiversity hotspot roughly the size of Florida
will be saved in perpetuity.

00:42:59.880 --> 00:43:04.160
Something that’s not going to last forever
is Filipino President Rodrigo Duterte, who

00:43:04.160 --> 00:43:07.020
is constitutionally barred from seeking another
six-year term.

00:43:07.020 --> 00:43:11.289
Ahead of the May elections, those looking
to replace him include a potpourri of people,

00:43:11.289 --> 00:43:15.320
including his daughter Sara Duterte; son and
namesake of the country’s former dictator,

00:43:15.320 --> 00:43:20.431
Ferdinand “Bongbong” Marcos; former actor
and Manila mayor Francisco Domagoso; and newly

00:43:20.431 --> 00:43:22.680
retired boxing star Manny Pacquiao.

00:43:22.680 --> 00:43:26.390
With candidates expected to perform onstage
with the public judging them on their singing

00:43:26.390 --> 00:43:30.799
and dancing abilities, the pool is certainly
high on the entertainment factor.

00:43:30.799 --> 00:43:36.270
To the south, the Indonesian island of Biak
is becoming an unexpected player in Elon Musk’s

00:43:36.270 --> 00:43:37.270
world.

00:43:37.270 --> 00:43:40.990
That’s because Indonesian president Joko
Widodo, as part of his goal to modernize the

00:43:40.990 --> 00:43:44.700
nation, personally pitched the tech giant
on launching spacecraft from the island that

00:43:44.700 --> 00:43:48.010
sits 70 miles or 110 kilometers, south of
the Equator.

00:43:48.010 --> 00:43:52.630
Facing displacement, the indigenous population
isn’t supportive of the proposal.

00:43:52.630 --> 00:43:57.740
Musk has also stayed mum on the issue, but
we all know his penchant for explosive announcements.

00:43:57.740 --> 00:44:02.090
Atmospheric conditions also made headlines
in Bolivia, where UV radiation levels in La

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:06.400
Paz hit 21, and that’s on a scale that typically
tops out at 20.

00:44:06.400 --> 00:44:11.020
The Bolivian capital sits at 11,800 feet,
or 3,600 meters, above sea level, and low

00:44:11.020 --> 00:44:15.260
levels of clouds caused by climate change
combined with a heat wave skyrocketed numbers

00:44:15.260 --> 00:44:16.559
on the UV index.

00:44:16.559 --> 00:44:20.609
The World Health Organization calls an index
of 11 “extreme," and 10 minutes exposed

00:44:20.609 --> 00:44:23.819
at these levels starts to burn the skin.

00:44:23.819 --> 00:44:25.839
At 21, people just stay inside.

00:44:25.839 --> 00:44:29.760
More than doubling the age of most of her
competitors, a 46-year-old gymnast representing

00:44:29.760 --> 00:44:33.330
Uzbekistan took her final bow at the Olympics
in Tokyo this summer.

00:44:33.330 --> 00:44:38.109
It was Oksana Chusovitina’s eighth Olympics;
she won the silver medal for vault in 2008,

00:44:38.109 --> 00:44:42.480
and capped off with 11 medals won at 17 world
championships.

00:44:42.480 --> 00:44:43.480
Up next?

00:44:43.480 --> 00:44:47.780
Spending more time with her Olympic wrestling
husband and her son, who at 22, is older than

00:44:47.780 --> 00:44:49.280
most Olympic gymnasts.

00:44:49.280 --> 00:44:53.500
In Libya, there’s some frightening news
on the artificial intelligence front.

00:44:53.500 --> 00:44:57.260
A report commissioned by the United Nations
found that a military drone strike used in

00:44:57.260 --> 00:45:00.530
the country’s civil war may have been carried
out autonomously.

00:45:00.530 --> 00:45:05.210
Allegedly, militia fighters were hunted down
and attacked by a “weapons system” that

00:45:05.210 --> 00:45:06.950
did not require data connectivity.

00:45:06.950 --> 00:45:11.500
Though it’s implied, the report doesn’t
go as far as to say the drone acted autonomously.

00:45:11.500 --> 00:45:15.849
Vagueness here is de rigueur, as the report
also doesn’t say whether anyone was injured

00:45:15.849 --> 00:45:16.920
or killed.

00:45:16.920 --> 00:45:20.770
The island-nation of Tonga managed to largely
avoid the global pandemic, but it’s dealing

00:45:20.770 --> 00:45:25.460
with its own epidemic now, as methamphetamine
use has become pervasive and very problematic

00:45:25.460 --> 00:45:26.860
in the country of 100,000.

00:45:26.860 --> 00:45:30.720
It’s so bad that the king made a plea to
Tongan parliament asking for help with the

00:45:30.720 --> 00:45:35.080
crisis, which is accounting for 60% of admissions
to Tonga’s mental health wards and filling

00:45:35.080 --> 00:45:36.420
up local prisons.

00:45:36.420 --> 00:45:40.500
Tonga has long been a stop on the drug route
from Asia to New Zealand and Australia—this

00:45:40.500 --> 00:45:44.329
just means that the drugs are increasingly
stopping instead of passing through.

00:45:44.329 --> 00:45:46.820
Something else that’s stopping is the Parana
River.

00:45:46.820 --> 00:45:51.230
South America’s second-largest river straddles
Paraguay, Argentina, and Brazil, and sadly,

00:45:51.230 --> 00:45:54.599
it’s the lowest it’s been in more than
70 years.

00:45:54.599 --> 00:45:58.569
Scientists say deforestation in the Amazon
is to blame, as rainfall, much of which emanates

00:45:58.569 --> 00:46:01.529
from the forest itself, has significantly
decreased.

00:46:01.529 --> 00:46:06.099
As a result, the river is 10.5 feet or 3 meters
lower than normal, and threatening the lives

00:46:06.099 --> 00:46:10.650
and businesses of people who populate more
than 60 towns along its shores.

00:46:10.650 --> 00:46:14.650
Also threatening was two mens’ plot to unseat
Jordan’s King Abdullah, a crime for which

00:46:14.650 --> 00:46:17.250
they were sentenced to 15 years in prison.

00:46:17.250 --> 00:46:20.660
They were aides to the king’s half-brother,
who was removed from the line of succession

00:46:20.660 --> 00:46:21.660
in 2004.

00:46:21.660 --> 00:46:26.109
But not to be deterred, his plan using these
proxies included rallying civilian support

00:46:26.109 --> 00:46:27.210
to oust Abdullah.

00:46:27.210 --> 00:46:28.430
The twist?

00:46:28.430 --> 00:46:32.460
Some say all three may have been unknowingly
backed by the Trump administration, who saw

00:46:32.460 --> 00:46:36.600
Abdullah as an obstacle in securing an Israeli-Palestinian
peace treaty.

00:46:36.600 --> 00:46:40.619
The king, however, will remain in power and
these two will go to jail.

00:46:40.619 --> 00:46:45.400
When an immigrant commits a crime in the United
Kingdom, they get deported, often by airplane.

00:46:45.400 --> 00:46:49.349
Lately, however, the loads have been light
with just four people sent back to Jamaica

00:46:49.349 --> 00:46:53.680
on a recent 350-passenger Airbus A350-900
flight.

00:46:53.680 --> 00:46:58.460
That evens out to about $66,000, or 50,000
pounds, per person.

00:46:58.460 --> 00:47:02.100
These costs have people wondering about the
viability of such an expensive program, while

00:47:02.100 --> 00:47:06.349
others say it’s critical to get convicted
criminals out of the country.

00:47:06.349 --> 00:47:10.700
Finds that will happily be sent overseas include
two of the world’s largest diamonds, unearthed

00:47:10.700 --> 00:47:12.070
this year in Botswana.

00:47:12.070 --> 00:47:18.570
A 1,174-carat and 1,098-carat diamond were
both discovered weeks apart in the country’s

00:47:18.570 --> 00:47:21.420
mines, which are some of the top-producing
in the world.

00:47:21.420 --> 00:47:25.420
Each diamond is about the size of someone’s
hand, and they come at an important time for

00:47:25.420 --> 00:47:29.240
the market which is trying to rebound after
the pandemic—bright news for the country

00:47:29.240 --> 00:47:31.180
and the soon-to-be betrothed.

00:47:31.180 --> 00:47:34.751
Another unearthing took place in Mongolia,
where archeologists were able to use a new

00:47:34.751 --> 00:47:39.370
technology to look below ground and map the
ancient city of Karakorum, the capital of

00:47:39.370 --> 00:47:43.049
the Mongolian Empire established by Genghis
Kahn in the 1200s.

00:47:43.049 --> 00:47:47.599
Using magnetic fields to determine the footprint’s
layout and structural composition, the researchers

00:47:47.599 --> 00:47:51.470
determined, among other things, that the city
was only at about 40% capacity, likely home

00:47:51.470 --> 00:47:55.950
to laborers and craftspeople, while the majority
lived a more nomadic lifestyle.

00:47:55.950 --> 00:47:57.250
And that brings us to halfway.

